+---
+id: dl-001-json-pointer-strict-rfc-6901-semantics-for-resolvepointer
+type: decision-log
+title: "JSON Pointer: strict RFC 6901 semantics for resolvePointer"
+status: pending
+format: 1
+---
+
+<!-- decision-log body. `wingfoil memory add` copies this scaffold, setting only `id`, `title` and
+     `status` (and any `--tags` or `--set` value). Fill the required frontmatter fields and replace
+     these placeholder comments yourself; `wingfoil memory submit` then checks the required fields,
+     moves `status` forward and commits the document as you left it, naming in the commit body the
+     content it carries. -->
+
+# Context
+
+Other features will build on `resolvePointer`, and the library must follow the published standard.
+RFC 6901 leaves some cases to the implementer's reading of the text.
+
+# Decision
+
+- The empty pointer returns the whole document; a pointer not starting with `/` is invalid.
+- `~` must be followed by `0` or `1`; any other use is an invalid pointer.
+- Array tokens must match `0|[1-9][0-9]*` (no leading zeros, no sign) and be in range.
+- `-` (past-the-end) never resolves, as it names a nonexistent element.
+- Object members are looked up as own properties only, so `__proto__`, `constructor` and
+  `length` never resolve through the prototype chain.
+- Both failure kinds throw a plain `Error`, distinguished by message only.
+
+# Consequences
+
+Strict behaviour is safe for later features (e.g. updates) to build on. Callers cannot tell
+"invalid" from "unresolved" except by message; a typed error can be added if needed.
